Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Ticket Stubs Bleed Brass
- Story & Context: When station supervisor Kester tried to clear the 03:42 Line 9 express at Terminal West, Mira shoved her sax bell against the emergency release valve to play a shrieking, triplet-swung blast that jammed the doors wide open. Insomniacs pulled out handheld samplers from their coats and locked into the screeching rhythm of the train's air compressor, drowning out security warnings while the carriage floor shook with low-battery 808 glides. Mira spat into the microphone as the doors failed to seal, howling "we punch the card until the copper dies" to a crowd of bewildered janitors and warehouse loaders.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic macro view of an iron subway turnstile covered in wet grit and trampled magnetic transit tickets, with miniature glowing synth circuit boards and brass saxophone valve oil spilling across the wet tile floor under harsh yellow station lighting.

Track 2: Why Did The Conductor Lock Carriage Six?
- Story & Context: Parked on a freezing siding track outside the Old Marshalling Yard, switchman Kaelen Cruz recorded his former supervisor's muffled frantic dispatch over the train radio while pacing the length of an abandoned postal wagon. Shivering under three layers of fleece, he overlaid the hollow clanking of loose freight chains with sweet, mournful Pluggnb chord swells and disjointed Charleston woodwind loops. As his battery meter flashed critical red, Kaelen leaned into a broken headset to croon "you locked the door because the cold has ears," immortalizing the panic of an operator stranded between automated switches.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Moody eye-level shot looking through the scratched safety glass of an interior train door into a pitch-black passenger car, illuminated only by the faint green LED indicators of a portable drum machine sitting on an empty vinyl seat.

Track 3: Yardmaster Vane’s Final Boarding
- Story & Context: According to transit legend, an ancient logistics clerk named Yardmaster Vane disappeared into the decommissioned North Spur tunnel, leaving behind only an uninterrupted four-track cassette tape loop of his morning schedule announcements. Bogie Flange Cabaret dragged a mobile generator down into the damp ballast bed of the North Spur to graft Vane's clipped vowels onto violently swung brass stabs and earth-shattering 808 slides. Splicing the clerk’s tape into their pocket samplers, the band roared the legendary refrain "the timetable ends where the switches rust" into the subterranean darkness while distant freight trains rattled the ceiling dust over their heads.
